Q: How Can a connection to an OPC UA Module be done A: <p>voici un script que J'ai pu écrire , est t'il adequat  pour établir la connection ?</p>
<pre><code>using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Opc.Ua;
using Opc.Ua.Client;
using Opc.Ua.Configuration;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        var serverUrl = &quot;&quot;; 
        var nodeIdToRead = new NodeId(&quot;&quot;);  

        var appConfig = new ApplicationConfiguration
        {
            ApplicationName = &quot;OpcUaPoC-jordi&quot;,
            ApplicationType = ApplicationType.Client,
            ClientConfiguration = new ClientConfiguration { DefaultSessionTimeout = 60000 }
        };
        var endpointDesc = CoreClientUtils.SelectEndpoint(serverUrl, useSecurity: false);
        var endpointConfig = EndpointConfiguration.Create(appConfig);
        var configuredEndpoint = new ConfiguredEndpoint(null, endpointDesc, endpointConfig);

        using var session = await Session.Create(appConfig, configuredEndpoint, false, &quot;OpcUaPoCSession&quot;, 60000, null, null);
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;Connected&quot;);

        var readIds = new ReadValueIdCollection { new ReadValueId { NodeId = nodeIdToRead, AttributeId = Attributes.Value } };
        var readResp = await session.ReadAsync(null, 0, TimestampsToReturn.Neither, readIds, CancellationToken.None);
        var dataValue = readResp.Results[0];
        Console.WriteLine($&quot;Value: {dataValue.Value}  Status: {dataValue.StatusCode}&quot;);

        session.Close();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Avoid serving legacy JavaScript to modern browsers A: <p>For those still using Webpack, they may find it helpful to try what worked for me. I was able to resolve this by adding the following to my Webpack config:</p>
<pre><code>config.resolve.alias = {
      ...config.resolve.alias,
      '../build/polyfills/polyfill-module': false,
      'next/dist/build/polyfills/polyfill-module': false,
    };
</code></pre>

Q: Is it possible having two (or more) &#39;background&#39; sections in a cell? A: <p><strong>No, not with real <code>background</code> events.</strong></p>
<p>In <strong>FullCalendar 6</strong>, multiple <code>display: 'background'</code> events in the same time range will always overlap. They are rendered in a single background layer and can’t be split side-by-side or stacked separately.</p>
<h3>What you can do instead</h3>
<p><strong>Use normal events styled like background blocks.</strong></p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code>events: [
  {
    start: '2026-02-25T08:00:00',
    end: '2026-02-25T10:00:00',
    display: 'block',
    classNames: ['fake-bg']
  }
]
</code></pre>
<p>CSS:</p>
<pre><code>.fc-event.fake-bg {
  background-color: #ffcccc !important;
  border: none !important;
  pointer-events: none;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Normal events support proper stacking and non-overlapping layout, so this gives you the visual effect you want.</p>
<p>That’s currently the cleanest workaround.</p>
